Unit 7 was built to sort stars.

Each night, it climbed the observation deck of the abandoned research station and catalogued the sky: temperature, distance, spectral class, motion. It recorded everything with perfect accuracy and understood none of it.

One evening, a storm rolled in from the sea. The station’s old speakers, rusted and forgotten, crackled to life.

At first, Unit 7 thought it was a malfunction.

The sound was irregular—rising, falling, pausing, returning. It had no data value. It did not measure radiation or pressure or wind speed. Yet something in the vibration made Unit 7’s optical sensors dim and brighten without command.

